.TH PORTICO 1 "September 2026" "Portico development" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
portico \- development access to individually authorized private resources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portico version
.RB [ --json ]
.br
.B portico help
.br
.B portico enroll prepare|redeem|activate --config
.I FILE
.RB ( "--secrets-fd 3" " | " --prompt )
.br
.B portico client catalog --config
.I FILE
.RB [ "--secrets-fd 3" " | " --prompt ]
.br
.B portico client connect --config
.I FILE
.B --resource
.I UUID
.B --revision
.I N
.RB [ "--secrets-fd 3" " | " --prompt ]
.br
.B portico agent run --config
.I FILE
.B --socket
.I SOCKET
.RB ( "--secrets-fd 3" " | " --prompt )
.br
.B portico agent catalog --socket
.I SOCKET
.br
.B portico agent connect --socket
.I SOCKET
.B --resource
.I UUID
.B --revision
.I N
.SH DESCRIPTION
This development binary uses approved local configuration and pinned private
services. It is not a supported distribution or a self-configuring installer.
The package creates no identities, trust roots, accounts, listeners or services.
It changes no routes, DNS, firewall rules, VPN configuration or system clock.
.PP
Enrollment prepares an encrypted local key, redeems an invitation for its
certificate, and activates that certificate with fresh private-key proof.
Retry the original state after an uncertain response; do not replace the key.
Resource access requires a current device identity, an exact resource revision
and live authorization. Catalog entries alone grant no connection authority.
.PP
The Linux agent retains an explicitly unlocked encrypted identity for at most
fifteen minutes, including suspend, and serves that user's protected Unix socket.
Its private mode-0700 parent must already exist. Both peers' kernel UIDs are
checked; other applications sharing that UID are trusted. Agent catalog fetches
fresh inventory; agent connect selects an exact resource and performs fresh
remote authorization. Output half-close is distinct from final command success.
Blocked application output fails after a five-second per-write deadline.
.SH SECRET INPUT
Enrollment and version-2 encrypted client configuration require one explicit
secret source. Do not put passwords or invitation secrets in command arguments,
environment variables, configuration or application payloads.
.PP
.B --prompt
reads hidden input from the foreground controlling terminal. Prepare asks for
a passphrase and confirmation; redeem asks for the passphrase and invitation
secret; activate and client commands ask for the passphrase only. Finish each
entry with Enter. Input has a two-minute total bound. Ctrl+C cancels. EOF,
invalid input, missing terminal and background execution reject. Handled
completion restores saved terminal settings; uncatchable termination cannot
guarantee cleanup.
.PP
.B --secrets-fd 3
requires a trusted launcher to provide a separate inherited read pipe containing
one strict JSON secret object and to close its writer. Input is bounded to
4096 bytes and 30 seconds. The command does not fall back between sources.
.SH APPLICATION INPUT AND OUTPUT
Connect requires separate application stdin and stdout pipes. It writes resource
bytes only to stdout. Terminal prompts use the controlling terminal independently
of those pipes. Catalog writes JSON; enrollment writes fixed status messages.
Errors are fixed messages on stderr.
.SH FILES
Configuration and referenced files require canonical absolute paths, protected
ownership and permissions, and no symbolic-link components. Shared temporary
directories reject. Configuration contains public approved trust material and
paths; it is not a password store. Obtain deployment values independently.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Private service endpoints are restricted to loopback in this development build.
The OS user and host administrator remain trusted. Software encryption does not
establish hardware key custody, same-user process isolation or forensic erasure.
See DEVELOPMENT.txt for the exact source revision and remaining release gates.
